  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quieter weather likely for the next several days.\n\n\nThe region is in for a relatively quiet period into the weekend and \nthrough next week. The trough that brought the previous batch of \nrain will move into the Atlantic later today and will \neventually merge with a secondary low off the east coast of the \nVA/NC border. Model guidance suggests there will be a brief \nperiod of the Fujiwhara Effect where the two lows will revolve \naround each other before combining by Saturday. This then\nnor'easter will remain close to the Mid-Atlantic until it \nfinally navigates to the north by Monday. The close proximity of\nthe system will result in northerly winds and dry and cool/mild\nweather today and Saturday. Early morning temperatures for \ntoday and Saturday will likely be in the 40s as a result with \nsome wind gusts of 15-25 mph in the Piedmont of Virginia and \nNorth Carolina Friday afternoon. Short term models are not too \ncertain how westward the nor'easter will be on Saturday, so \ngusty winds are not out of the question for the weekend. The \nmid-level component of the storm system will send energy through\nthe eastern CONUS but the air will be too dry for any \nprecipitation development. \"Worse\" case scenario is likely some\nperiods of cloudy skies in between clear sky conditions.\n\nThe nor'easter is expected to be far away from us by the middle of \nnext week and a ridge over the Texas/Louisiana border is still \nforecast to move towards Mexico. This will result in a break in the \nnortherly winds and a transition to westerly. Long range model \nguidance continues to show a trough progressing through central \nCONUS and potentially reaching the east coast by late next week. The \ntiming of this is uncertain and a high pressure system over Bermuda \nthat replaces the nor'easter may block the trough entirely. Still, \nthe overall weather trend looks to be an increase in temperatures \nand dew points by the end of the forecast period.",
